The model is competitive screening (Rothschild-Stiglitz style): homeowners compete, so any offered contract must satisfy the break-even condition \(p=v\) while maximizing the handyman's payoff subject to IC and IR. This forces the only feasible payoff for type \(H\) to be exactly zero. The resulting candidate separating pair is the pair of full-information zero-profit contracts \((v_L=1/2,p_L=1/2)\) and \((v_H,p_H=v_H)\).  

These violate the IC constraint for \(H\) (who strictly prefers the \(L\) contract) at the given prior \(\frac56\). Hence no separating equilibrium exists. (The \(\theta_L(v)\) dependence and competition only reinforce that conclusion by further distorting outside options and violating single-crossing.)

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {no separating equilibrium exists}

